// SWARM RABBIT HOLE — Wave 8 (Impl-L): GC scoping — the E-GC-IN-LANE
// fail case, GC→lane direction. D0 gap #6: GC roots are globals+stacks+
// compiler-pushed values only (runtime.c) — a GC-managed pointer stored
// into lane memory is invisible to the collector and can be swept while
// live. W2A §1.4's memory categories: lane bytes (sn_lane_alloc —
// untracked mmap, never gc_track'd) and the GC heap (sn_obj_new →
// gc_track) have different lifetimes and neither collector knows about
// the other, so the category confusion is a checked error, not a
// runtime hazard.
//
// 'p' is GC-managed (the `new` constructor path); 'lane_box' is
// lane-backed (the explicit `lane_alloc` pin, lane_of). Storing a ref
// to 'p' into 'lane_box' must fire E-GC-IN-LANE at the T-STORE site.
// The single-region ⊑ₗ poset cannot see this: both bindings sit in the
// same lexical region (region 0), so E-ESCAPE stays silent — only the
// category rule rejects it.
//
// Compile-FAIL golden case (tests/golden.rs): [E-GC-IN-LANE].

blueprint Point {
    int x
    int y
}

fn main() {
    new Point p(x: 1, y: 2)
    ptr lane_box = lane_alloc(2, 64)
    lane_box = address(p)
}
